### Drill 2: StorageClass Authoring & Parameter Mechanics (`02-storage-classes/`)

#### Critical CKA Exam Note

Kubernetes has **no** `kubectl create storageclass` or `kubectl create sc` CLI command. You must write the StorageClass
manifest manually or quickly check the schema with `kubectl explain storageclass`.

#### Core StorageClass Parameters

- **`provisioner`**: The internal or CSI driver responsible for provisioning volumes (e.g. `rancher.io/local-path`,
  `nfs.csi.k8s.io`, `ebs.csi.aws.com`).
- **`reclaimPolicy`**: Determines what happens to dynamically created PVs when their PVC is deleted (`Delete` by
  default, or `Retain` to preserve data).
- **`volumeBindingMode`**:
  - `Immediate`: PV is provisioned and bound as soon as the PVC is created, regardless of whether any Pod references it.
  - `WaitForFirstConsumer`: PV provisioning and binding are delayed until a Pod using the PVC is scheduled. This ensures
    the volume is provisioned on the specific node where the Pod actually runs.
- **`allowVolumeExpansion`**: When set to `true`, permits increasing the storage capacity of bound PVCs via live
  manifest patching.

#### CKA Exam Shortcut: Managing the Default StorageClass

A common CKA exam objective requires designating a StorageClass as the cluster default or switching defaults. This is
controlled via the `storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class` annotation:

```bash
# Mark a StorageClass as the default provisioner
kubectl patch storageclass cka-lab-sc-delete-wait -p '{"metadata":{"annotations":{"storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class":"true"}}}'

# Verify the default marker (shows (default) next to the name)
kubectl get storageclass

# Remove default status from a StorageClass
kubectl patch storageclass cka-lab-sc-delete-wait -p '{"metadata":{"annotations":{"storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class":"false"}}}'
```

#### Step 1: Manually Author a StorageClass with `Retain` & `Immediate`

Create a StorageClass where volumes are provisioned immediately and retained upon claim deletion.
A ready-to-use manifest `manual-sc-retain.yaml` (also available as `cka-lab-sc-retain.yaml`) is pre-staged in this
directory, or you can author it manually:

```bash
cd {{ cka_lab_storage_classes_dir }}/02-storage-classes

# Apply pre-staged manifest directly (or author manually):
kubectl apply -f manual-sc-retain.yaml
```

Manifest definition (`manual-sc-retain.yaml`):

```yaml
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: cka-lab-sc-retain
provisioner: rancher.io/local-path
reclaimPolicy: Retain
volumeBindingMode: Immediate
allowVolumeExpansion: true
```

#### Step 2: Manually Author a StorageClass with `Delete` & `WaitForFirstConsumer`

Create a StorageClass demonstrating delayed binding and automated cleanup.
A ready-to-use manifest `manual-sc-delete-wait.yaml` (also available as `cka-lab-sc-delete-wait.yaml`) is pre-staged in
this directory:

```bash
# Apply pre-staged manifest directly (or author manually):
kubectl apply -f manual-sc-delete-wait.yaml
kubectl get storageclass
```

Manifest definition (`manual-sc-delete-wait.yaml`):

```yaml
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: cka-lab-sc-delete-wait
provisioner: rancher.io/local-path
reclaimPolicy: Delete
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
allowVolumeExpansion: true
```

