### Drill 3: Dynamic PVC Lifecycle, Delayed Binding & Volume Expansion (`02-storage-classes/`)

#### Step 1: Manually Create the Dynamic PVC

Create a claim requesting 100Mi using the delayed-binding StorageClass.
A ready-to-use manifest `manual-dynamic-pvc.yaml` (also available as `cka-lab-dynamic-pvc.yaml`) is pre-staged in this
directory, or you can author it manually:

```bash
cd {{ cka_lab_storage_classes_dir }}/02-storage-classes

# Apply pre-staged manifest directly (or author manually):
kubectl apply -f manual-dynamic-pvc.yaml
```

Manifest definition (`manual-dynamic-pvc.yaml`):

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: cka-lab-dynamic-pvc
  namespace: {{ cka_lab_storage_classes_namespace | default('default') }}
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  storageClassName: cka-lab-sc-delete-wait
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 100Mi
```

#### Step 2: Inspect Delayed Binding Mechanics (`Pending` Phase)

Check the PVC status immediately after creation:

```bash
kubectl get pvc cka-lab-dynamic-pvc -n {{ cka_lab_storage_classes_namespace | default('default') }}
kubectl describe pvc cka-lab-dynamic-pvc -n {{ cka_lab_storage_classes_namespace | default('default') }}
```

Notice that the PVC remains in the `Pending` phase. Look at the Events section:

```text
Waiting for first consumer to be created before binding
```

Because `volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer` was configured on `cka-lab-sc-delete-wait`, Kubernetes deliberately
postpones provisioning until a Pod using this claim is scheduled, ensuring storage is allocated on the node where the
consumer will execute.

#### Step 3: Create the Consumer Pod to Trigger Automatic PV Provisioning

A ready-to-use manifest `consumer-pod.yaml` (also available as `cka-lab-consumer-pod.yaml`) is pre-staged in this
directory:

```bash
kubectl apply -f consumer-pod.yaml
kubectl wait --for=condition=Ready pod/cka-lab-storage-consumer -n {{ cka_lab_storage_classes_namespace | default('default') }} --timeout=60s

# Verify that both PVC and dynamically generated PV are now Bound
kubectl get pvc cka-lab-dynamic-pvc -n {{ cka_lab_storage_classes_namespace | default('default') }}
kubectl get pv
```

Or generate the pod skeleton imperatively and attach the PVC manually:

```bash
kubectl run cka-lab-storage-consumer \
  --image=busybox:1.36.1 \
  --restart=Always \
  --namespace={{ cka_lab_storage_classes_namespace | default('default') }} \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml \
  -- /bin/sh -c "echo 'DYNAMIC_STORAGE_VALIDATED=true' > /data/status.txt && sleep 3600" > consumer-pod.yaml
```

Attach the volume in `consumer-pod.yaml`:

```yaml
spec:
  containers:
    - name: writer
      image: busybox:1.36.1
      command: ["/bin/sh", "-c", "echo 'DYNAMIC_STORAGE_VALIDATED=true' > /data/status.txt && sleep 3600"]
      volumeMounts:
        - name: app-storage
          mountPath: /data
  volumes:
    - name: app-storage
      persistentVolumeClaim:
        claimName: cka-lab-dynamic-pvc
```

Observe that a PersistentVolume named `pvc-<uuid>` was automatically created by the provisioner and bound to
`cka-lab-dynamic-pvc`.

Verify container storage persistence:

```bash
kubectl exec -n {{ cka_lab_storage_classes_namespace | default('default') }} cka-lab-storage-consumer -- cat /data/status.txt
```

#### Step 4: Live PVC Volume Expansion (`100Mi` -> `200Mi`)

In Kubernetes, PVC storage capacity can be expanded dynamically if the backing StorageClass has `allowVolumeExpansion:
true`:

- **Two-Phase Architecture**:
  1. **API Admission Phase (`spec`)**: The API server validates that the StorageClass has `allowVolumeExpansion: true`.
     If valid, it updates `.spec.resources.requests.storage`.
  2. **Controller Resizing Phase (`status`)**: An external CSI resizer sidecar (`csi-resizer`) expands the backing
     storage and updates `.status.capacity.storage`.
- **One-Way Direction**: You can only **increase** storage size; reducing PVC capacity is strictly rejected by the API
  server.
- **In-Place Mutation**: You do not need to delete the PVC or terminate the consumer Pod.

Patch the PVC to request 200Mi:

```bash
kubectl patch pvc cka-lab-dynamic-pvc \
  -n {{ cka_lab_storage_classes_namespace | default('default') }} \
  -p '{"spec":{"resources":{"requests":{"storage":"200Mi"}}}}'
```

Verify that the API server accepted the updated requested capacity in `.spec`:

```bash
# Query the updated spec request (shows 200Mi):
kubectl get pvc cka-lab-dynamic-pvc \
  -n {{ cka_lab_storage_classes_namespace | default('default') }} \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.resources.requests.storage}'
echo ""

# Compare spec (requested) vs status (controller-reported):
kubectl get pvc cka-lab-dynamic-pvc \
  -n {{ cka_lab_storage_classes_namespace | default('default') }} \
  -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,REQUESTED_SPEC:.spec.resources.requests.storage,STATUS_CAPACITY:.status.capacity.storage
```

> [!NOTE]
> **Understanding Provisioner Behavior: `local-path-provisioner` vs. Full CSI Drivers**:
>
> - Rancher `local-path-provisioner` maps directories on the node's host filesystem (`hostPath`). It is **not** a
>   block-device CSI driver and does not run a `csi-resizer` controller. Therefore, `.spec.resources.requests.storage`
>   updates to `200Mi` immediately (validating the CKA exam objective for `allowVolumeExpansion`), while
>   `.status.capacity.storage` remains at `100Mi` because host directory storage has no block device resizing agent.
> - The container has instant access to all available disk space on the host node filesystem regardless of the requested
>   figure.
> - For full end-to-end CSI volume expansion where both `.status.capacity.storage` and container `df -h` resize live,
>   refer to the Ceph RBD drill at
>   [`~/cka/12-volumes/06-ceph/04-rbd-pvc-expansion.yaml`](../12-volumes/06-ceph/README.md) and Fibre Channel SAN drill
>   at [`~/cka/12-volumes/08-fc/06-fc-lun-expansion.yaml`](../12-volumes/08-fc/README.md).

Verify the **One-Way Direction** restriction by attempting to shrink the PVC to 50Mi:

```bash
# This mutation will be strictly rejected by the API server:
kubectl patch pvc cka-lab-dynamic-pvc \
  -n {{ cka_lab_storage_classes_namespace | default('default') }} \
  -p '{"spec":{"resources":{"requests":{"storage":"50Mi"}}}}'
```

Observe the error response from the API server:

```text
The PersistentVolumeClaim "cka-lab-dynamic-pvc" is invalid: spec.resources.requests.storage: Forbidden: field can not be less than previous value
```

